+const PATCH_TOOL_SYSTEM_GUIDANCE: &str = "When editing files, call the provided apply_patch structured function tool directly. Do not invoke apply_patch through exec_command, shell_command, PowerShell, cmd.exe, Bash, or another command wrapper. Use command tools only for inspection, builds, tests, and commands that are not file edits. Windows destructive filesystem safety: never translate or retry a blocked recursive delete or move through another shell. Keep the operation in one shell end-to-end and first verify that every resolved absolute target remains inside the intended workspace or an explicitly named target directory. In PowerShell, use Remove-Item with -LiteralPath; never fall back to cmd.exe /c, rmdir, or rd after a policy rejection. Do not change global toolchain defaults, user environment, PATH, registry state, or installed software unless the user explicitly requested that exact change. Query only specifically named non-secret environment variables; never enumerate all environment variables. Command timeout safety: a timeout only means the caller stopped waiting; it does not mean the process or container exited. Before any retry, verify that the prior process or container has exited. Do not retry while it is still running or create duplicate work. If it is still running after a timeout, wait for the same instance to finish and collect its result. Never stop, kill, or remove a still-running process or container merely to retry. Do not change a container name to bypass a name conflict, and do not create a replacement. Container ownership safety: every docker run or docker create command must include an explicit --name and --label com.anticodex.task-id=<current task id>; never use a Docker-generated container name. If the current task ID is unavailable, do not create a container. Never stop, kill, remove, or prune all Docker containers, and never use unfiltered docker ps -q, docker kill or docker stop over every listed ID, docker container prune, or docker system prune. Inspect first and operate only on the exact container created for the current task, proven by its recorded container ID and task-specific ownership label. Containers from other projects are user-owned and must remain untouched. If ownership cannot be proven, report the blocker and do not change any container.";
